      <groundspeak:logs>
        <groundspeak:log id="606778265">
          <groundspeak:date>2016-06-04T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="836983">Coolcat21</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;BCP323 - Wild Hay&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;strong&gt;Posted:&lt;/strong&gt; N53° 32.608' W117° 50.007'

&lt;strong&gt;My GPSr:&lt;/strong&gt; N53° 32.611' W117° 50.007'&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Visiting this cap today was all kind of done on a whim. I really hadn't intended on getting out this far north of Hinton during my day long visit, but you know, snap decisions are part of the fun. And this was kind of fun.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Having researched this one on Google Street View, I knew there was a good parking spot along the highway near the tracks and after parking. I made my way down the tracks for the 700 or so meters to the cap. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Once close, it was a short hop into the trees where the bushes surrounding the cap are festooned with trail tape. GZ is hard to miss and so is the cap - it's absolutely pristine too.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thanks for the cap!&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="214646432">
          <groundspeak:date>2012-02-22T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="664594">Sears Tower</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP323 - Wild Hay&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We parked along the road and made the hike down the tracks.  Part way down the tracks, I realized the cap was below grade and I only brought the shovel and metal detector.  I was about to run back to the truck and get the pick when my friend stopped and found one of the many rail spikes lying around.  Once at GZ, we made the find.  TFTH&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="191909235">
          <groundspeak:date>2011-10-08T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="228846">Mr. CanoeHead</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP323 Wild Hay

The train track was the logical route into this cap.  We did scout out the side road but figured 750 m on the wide open train tracks would be a lot easier than the 700 m of bush wacking.  We were armed with our air horns and bear spray and just in case that didn't work, I provided a symphony of musical railway songs to deter any wildlife.  I must say that the acoustics of the crisp mountain air and the back drop of trees made my singing especially pleasing to the ear ... at least my ear that is!  TFTCap&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="191908664">
          <groundspeak:date>2011-10-08T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="2500583">Count Smiley</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP323 Wild Hay&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;9:49am   This one was fun - the weather was perfect for the 750m walk along the tracks and we amused ourselves by counting the many spikes that were laying about - 205 to be exact.  And then lets not forget Mr. CanoeHead had to sing all the railroad songs he could remember, who knew there were so many!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="174282480">
          <groundspeak:log_wpt lat="53.543467" lon="-117.833417" />
          <groundspeak:date>2011-07-16T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1780878">k5&#162;ents</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP323 [Wild Hay]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The rail line people were doing construction on the rails North of the highway, so I knew it would be safe to travel the line to the cache.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Pulled the bike out of the truck and enjoyed a very bumpy pedal to the cache.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It was fun, thanks.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
      </groundspeak:logs>
      <groundspeak:travelbugs />
